READING
  `error spread` is the quantity that matters, in units of the region's
  own best error. Non-zero means the bootstrap picked arms with
  different errors at that exchange rate, so a rerun of the bench would
  change the numeric answer the shipped program produces. Zero means the
  hazard does not fire at that rate, whatever the time column did.

  The control must report zero spread at every rate: its two arms agree
  to twelve printed digits, so no flip between them can change an answer.
  A non-zero spread there would mean this probe is measuring wrong.
